  "attributionNote": "Written by Ramanuja as an independent summary of his reading of the Vedanta, before or beside his commentary on the Brahma Sutras.",
  "description": "The Vedarthasamgraha gathers the meaning of the Vedanta into a single argument: that the Upanishads teach one reality, Brahman, which is the self of all things and is known through Scripture; that the individual soul and the material world are real modes of it, not illusions; and that devotion, understood as uninterrupted remembrance informed by knowledge, is the means of release. It is the compact statement of Vishishtadvaita.",
  "historicalContext": "Ramanuja's teaching became the philosophical foundation of the Shri Vaishnava tradition and one of the three great schools of Vedanta; the Vedarthasamgraha is the shortest way into it, and was written for those who had studied the Upanishads but wanted their sense drawn together.",
